// toolchain-policy.buzz - the version windows this workspace requires of the binaries
// its spells drive, shared by the projects that opt into them.
//
// A module rather than an export on the root magusfile, for two reasons. The practical
// one: `import "project/.." as root` exposes only a project's `export fun` targets, read
// statically from the AST so an import can never trigger a VM load and recurse - so an
// exported VALUE there reads as null and the key is silently skipped. The better one:
// a policy several projects share is a shared module, which is how tools/audit.buzz is
// already shared by console, docs, and evals. Nothing here is inherited by position in
// the tree; a project that wants these writes the import.
//
// Each window is an inclusive `min` and an exclusive `below`. Both are plain versions,
// not a constraint range - see spells.VersionBounds for why the range language was
// deliberately not offered.
//
// magus never learns which versions exist upstream and never selects one. It probes the
// binary that actually ran and compares against these, so moving a bound is always a
// person's edit.

// go tracks mise.toml's pin. No ceiling: nothing here is known to break on a newer Go,
// and a ceiling nobody has verified fails builds for a bound that was never true.
export final go = {"min": "1.26"};

// node's floor tracks mise.toml (`node = "24"`, so 22 is the oldest still sane) and the
// ceiling says 25 has not been qualified against this tree - NOT that it cannot work.
// Raising it is a deliberate act after someone checks.
export final node = {"min": "22", "below": "25"};

// all is the whole set, for a project that drives several toolchains. A project that
// drives one should import the one - a node-only project declaring a go window states a
// requirement it does not have, and would fail on a Go nobody there uses.
export final all = {"go": go, "node": node};
